import mundane theorem    stdlib/geometry/distance-of-points-by-coordinates

theorem right-isosceles-triangle
  note        Each side is read off the coordinates. The long side squared is
              50, and so is the sum of the squares of the two short ones.
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume A = (−2, −1)                                                 (H4)
  assume B = (2, 2)                                                   (H5)
  assume C = (5, −2)                                                  (H6)
  then |AB| = 5. |BC| = 5. |AC|² = |AB|² + |BC|².

1.  |AB| = 5
    calculation
      |AB| = √((−2 − 2)² + (−1 − 2)²)     mun:distance-of-points-by-coordinates, from H4, H5
           = √25                          arithmetic
           = 5                            real arithmetic

2.  |BC| = 5
    calculation
      |BC| = √((2 − 5)² + (2 − (−2))²)    mun:distance-of-points-by-coordinates, from H5, H6
           = √25                          arithmetic
           = 5                            real arithmetic

3.  |AC| = √((−2 − 5)² + (−1 − (−2))²)
    mun:distance-of-points-by-coordinates, from H4, H6

4.  |AB|² + |BC|² = 5² + |BC|²
    substitute |AB| = 5 (line 1)

5.  |AB|² + |BC|² = 5² + 5²
    substitute |BC| = 5 (line 2) into line 4

6.  |AC|² = |AB|² + |BC|²
    calculation
      |AC|² = (√((−2 − 5)² + (−1 − (−2))²))²     substitute line 3
            = 50                                 real arithmetic
            = 5² + 5²                            arithmetic
            = |AB|² + |BC|²                      5

7.  |AB| = 5. |BC| = 5. |AC|² = |AB|² + |BC|².
    join 1, 2, 6
